  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in Iowa totaled $1.4B in obligations in FY2026 to date. The Federal Government issued 19,029 awards in the state, with an average award size of $71.3K. Obligations fell 62.3% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a peak and recent decline: FY21 was $2.5B, FY22 was $2.7B, FY23 was $3.4B, FY24 was $3B, FY25 was $3.6B, and FY26 is $1.4B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state with $868M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Other large buying agencies include the Department of the Treasury with $175M and the Department of Agriculture with $105M. Other notable industries by obligation are Other Communications Equipment Manufacturing at $264M and Copper Rolling, Drawing, Extruding, and Alloying at $116M. The vendor list is led by ROCKWELL COLLINS, INC. with $310M and followed by DATA LINK SOLUTIONS LLC with $254M. This snapshot covers partial current-year totals because FY2026 is fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year decline?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $868M in FY2026 to date, but overall obligations fell 62.3% year over year. Total obligations in FY26 are $1.4B, down from $3.6B in FY25. The drop in obligations occurred across multiple agencies and major vendors, contributing to the lower statewide total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and agencies account for most spending in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $868M, followed by the Department of the Treasury with $175M and the Department of Agriculture with $105M. Other Communications Equipment Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligation at $264M, followed by Copper Rolling, Drawing, Extruding, and Alloying at $116M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $112M. Several construction and research NAICS categories also show notable obligations in the state."
      }
    ]
  },
